\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} Optimal bit-widths from reverse water-filling at a 3-bit average budget (synthetic Gaussian logits, measured cost constants). As logit spread $\tau $ grows the profile goes from near-uniform to a ladder to keep/evict. \textbf  {(b)} \emph  {Measured}: routed gain of allocation over the better of uniform quantization and eviction, per (model, context); marker size grows with context and arrows mark the last doubling. One derived variable orders all 24 points ($\rho =-0.96$), and every model moves right as context grows. \textbf  {(c)} \emph  {Projected, not measured} ({\color  {tbdcol}\textbf  {TBD}\nobreakspace  {}[R8]}): the end-task picture the phase diagram predicts, with the largest gain at short-to-mid context, shrinking toward eviction at 128k.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{2}{\textbf {(a)} Optimal bit-widths from reverse water-filling at a 3-bit average budget (synthetic Gaussian logits, measured cost constants). As logit spread $\tau $ grows the profile goes from near-uniform to a ladder to keep/evict. \textbf {(b)} \emph {Measured}: routed gain of allocation over the better of uniform quantization and eviction, per (model, context); marker size grows with context and arrows mark the last doubling. One derived variable orders all 24 points ($\rho =-0.96$), and every model moves right as context grows. \textbf {(c)} \emph {Projected, not measured} (\tbd {[R8]}): the end-task picture the phase diagram predicts, with the largest gain at short-to-mid context, shrinking toward eviction at 128k}{figure.1}{}}
